    "q-163278": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163278",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221067/assets/f84a8fcd4ed668a6b94d5b084ade49dc8fc027feff4baea862e2feaf68221534.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the semicircle shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(-1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{[-\\frac{{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}({cos}^{5}⁡\\pi -{cos}^{5}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{8}{5\\pi }.\n$$",
